!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math::Real-Numbers
!tags: Math Real-Numbers Bounds Extrema

!id: UnulCNcJun
!front: What does it mean for a real number \( M \) to be an upper bound of a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \)?
!back:
\( M \) is an upper bound of \( A \) if every element of \( A \) is at most \( M \):
\[ \forall a \in A,\quad a \le M. \]

The set \( A \) is bounded above if it has at least one real upper bound.
~~~

!id: B4Fb4t8vP3
!front: What does it mean for a real number \( m \) to be a lower bound of a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \)?
!back:
\( m \) is a lower bound of \( A \) if every element of \( A \) is at least \( m \):
\[ \forall a \in A,\quad m \le a. \]

The set \( A \) is bounded below if it has at least one real lower bound.
~~~

!id: wHP9AJwzKI
!front: What does it mean for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) to be bounded?
!back:
\( A \) is bounded if it is both bounded above and bounded below.

Equivalently, there exist real numbers \( m \) and \( M \) such that
\[ m \le a \le M \]
for every \( a \in A \).
!related: This is equivalent to the absolute-value form: there exists \( C \ge 0 \) such that \( |a| \le C \) for every \( a \in A \).
~~~

!id: K8JdSvjQOk
!front: Define \( \sup A \) for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
A real number \( s \) is the supremum of \( A \), written \( s=\sup A \), if:

- \( s \) is an upper bound of \( A \);
- every upper bound \( M \) of \( A \) satisfies \( s \le M \).

So \( \sup A \) is the least upper bound of \( A \).
~~~

!id: mQCVqBQcrb
!front: Define \( \inf A \) for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
A real number \( t \) is the infimum of \( A \), written \( t=\inf A \), if:

- \( t \) is a lower bound of \( A \);
- every lower bound \( m \) of \( A \) satisfies \( m \le t \).

So \( \inf A \) is the greatest lower bound of \( A \).
~~~

!id: LaoDQSIigh
!front: Compare an upper bound of \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) with \( \sup A \).
!back:
- An upper bound is any real number \( M \) with \( a \le M \) for every \( a \in A \).
- The supremum \( \sup A \), if it exists, is the smallest of all upper bounds.

So a set can have many upper bounds but at most one supremum.

![A bounded real set with its least upper bound, greatest lower bound, and looser bound candidates](img/sup-inf-anatomy.svg)
~~~

!id: Xu8s9H88Mg
!front: Compare a lower bound of \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) with \( \inf A \).
!back:
- A lower bound is any real number \( m \) with \( m \le a \) for every \( a \in A \).
- The infimum \( \inf A \), if it exists, is the largest of all lower bounds.

So a set can have many lower bounds but at most one infimum.

![A bounded real set with its least upper bound, greatest lower bound, and looser bound candidates](img/sup-inf-anatomy.svg)
~~~

!id: Di3Cg1J5Pa
!front: Give the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( s=\sup A \) for \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
\( s=\sup A \) if and only if:

- \( s \) is an upper bound of \( A \);
- for every \( \varepsilon>0 \), there exists \( a \in A \) such that
  \[ s-\varepsilon < a \le s. \]

The second condition says that elements of \( A \) occur at \( s \) or arbitrarily close below \( s \).
~~~

!id: cv4bF9Q0Yi
!front: Give the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( t=\inf A \) for \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
\( t=\inf A \) if and only if:

- \( t \) is a lower bound of \( A \);
- for every \( \varepsilon>0 \), there exists \( a \in A \) such that
  \[ t \le a < t+\varepsilon. \]

The second condition says that elements of \( A \) occur at \( t \) or arbitrarily close above \( t \).
~~~

!id: CIfe12gUsc
!front: Why is the upper-bound condition essential in the \( \varepsilon \)-characterization of \( \sup A \)?
!back:
The condition
\[ \forall \varepsilon>0,\ \exists a \in A \text{ with } s-\varepsilon<a \]
only says \( A \) comes arbitrarily close above \( s-\varepsilon \). It does not say \( s \) lies above all of \( A \).

For example, with \( A=(0,1) \), the number \( s=0 \) satisfies this closeness condition, but \( 0 \) is not an upper bound of \( A \), so \( 0 \ne \sup A \).
~~~

!id: hXgTQkoraH
!front: Prove that a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) has at most one supremum.
!back:
Let:

- \( s \) and \( t \) are both suprema of \( A \).

To prove: \( s=t \).

- Since \( s \) is the least upper bound and \( t \) is an upper bound, \( s \le t \).
- Since \( t \) is the least upper bound and \( s \) is an upper bound, \( t \le s \).
- Therefore \( s=t \).
~~~

!id: p5faBkXMtk
!front: Prove that a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) has at most one infimum.
!back:
Let:

- \( s \) and \( t \) are both infima of \( A \).

To prove: \( s=t \).

- Since \( s \) is the greatest lower bound and \( t \) is a lower bound, \( t \le s \).
- Since \( t \) is the greatest lower bound and \( s \) is a lower bound, \( s \le t \).
- Therefore \( s=t \).
~~~

!id: yMScVQLBbX
!front: How are \( \max A \) and \( \sup A \) related for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \)?
!back:
\( \max A \) is an element of \( A \) that is at least every element of \( A \).

If \( \max A \) exists, then
\[ \sup A = \max A. \]

Conversely, if \( \sup A \) exists and \( \sup A \in A \), then \( \max A \) exists and equals \( \sup A \).
~~~

!id: h43InUfDAO
!front: How are \( \min A \) and \( \inf A \) related for a set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \)?
!back:
\( \min A \) is an element of \( A \) that is at most every element of \( A \).

If \( \min A \) exists, then
\[ \inf A = \min A. \]

Conversely, if \( \inf A \) exists and \( \inf A \in A \), then \( \min A \) exists and equals \( \inf A \).
~~~

!id: HuctxZBdwo
!front: Compare maximum, supremum, minimum, and infimum for \( (0,1) \) and \( [0,1] \).
!back:
- For \( (0,1) \): \( \inf(0,1)=0 \) and \( \sup(0,1)=1 \), but there is no minimum or maximum because neither endpoint belongs to the set.
- For \( [0,1] \): \( \min[0,1]=\inf[0,1]=0 \) and \( \max[0,1]=\sup[0,1]=1 \), because both endpoints belong to the set.

![Open interval endpoints give an infimum and supremum not attained, while closed interval endpoints give a minimum and maximum](img/interval-attainment.svg)
~~~

!id: Jw3XpYtNcQ
!front: Does every nonempty finite subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) have a maximum and minimum?
!back:
Yes. Every nonempty finite subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) has both a maximum and a minimum, so
\[ \sup A=\max A \quad\text{and}\quad \inf A=\min A \]
always hold for finite \( A \).
!extra: This contrasts with infinite sets, where attainment can fail even when the set is bounded — e.g. \( \sup(0,1)=1 \) is never attained, since \( (0,1) \) is infinite.
~~~

!id: bRPnlB2lAL
!front: Give an example of a subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) whose supremum exists but whose maximum does not.
!back:
One example is
\[ A = \left\{-\frac{1}{n}: n \in \mathbb{N},\ n\ge 1\right\}. \]

Then \( \sup A = 0 \), since all elements are negative and approach \( 0 \) from below. But \( 0 \notin A \), so \( A \) has no maximum.

![The set {-1/n : n in N} approaches 0 from below, so 0 is the supremum but is not a maximum](img/sup-without-max.svg)
~~~

!id: 5Ta2bjvCjc
!front: Give an example of a subset of \( \mathbb{R} \) whose infimum exists but whose minimum does not.
!back:
One example is
\[ A=(0,1). \]

Then \( \inf A=0 \), since \( 0 \) is the greatest lower bound. But \( 0 \notin A \), so \( A \) has no minimum.
~~~

!id: 4dTece60nH
!front: For \( a<b \), compute \( \inf \), \( \sup \), minimum, and maximum for the four bounded intervals \( (a,b) \), \( [a,b] \), \( (a,b] \), and \( [a,b) \).
!back:
For all four intervals, the infimum is \( a \) and the supremum is \( b \).

Endpoint attainment determines minimum and maximum:

- \( (a,b) \): no minimum, no maximum.
- \( [a,b] \): minimum \( a \), maximum \( b \).
- \( (a,b] \): no minimum, maximum \( b \).
- \( [a,b) \): minimum \( a \), no maximum.
~~~

!id: KfiSGgOysP
!front: For \( a \in \mathbb{R} \), compute bounds and extrema for \( (a,\infty) \) and \( [a,\infty) \).
!back:
- \( (a,\infty) \) has \( \inf(a,\infty)=a \), no minimum, no real upper bound, and no supremum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
- \( [a,\infty) \) has \( \min[a,\infty)=\inf[a,\infty)=a \), no real upper bound, and no supremum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
!extra: In extended-real notation one may write \( \sup(a,\infty)=+\infty \), but \( +\infty \) is not a real number and is not a supremum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
~~~

!id: O4Mkk1MTbc
!front: For \( b \in \mathbb{R} \), compute bounds and extrema for \( (-\infty,b) \) and \( (-\infty,b] \).
!back:
- \( (-\infty,b) \) has \( \sup(-\infty,b)=b \), no maximum, no real lower bound, and no infimum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
- \( (-\infty,b] \) has \( \max(-\infty,b]=\sup(-\infty,b]=b \), no real lower bound, and no infimum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
!extra: In extended-real notation one may write \( \inf(-\infty,b)=-\infty \), but \( -\infty \) is not a real number and is not an infimum in \( \mathbb{R} \).
~~~

!id: AWrfHH3zQ9
!front: What upper and lower bounds does the empty set \( \emptyset \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) have?
!back:
Every real number is both an upper bound and a lower bound of \( \emptyset \), because the condition "for every \( a \in \emptyset \)" is vacuously true.

But \( \emptyset \) has no supremum or infimum in \( \mathbb{R} \): there is no least real number among all upper bounds, and no greatest real number among all lower bounds.
!extra: This is why completeness theorems normally require the set to be nonempty.
~~~

!id: WoBCoWGNqf
!front: If \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) is nonempty, bounded below, and bounded above, why must \( \inf A \le \sup A \)?
!back:
Choose any \( a \in A \), possible because \( A \) is nonempty.

Since \( \inf A \) is a lower bound and \( \sup A \) is an upper bound,
\[ \inf A \le a \le \sup A. \]

Therefore \( \inf A \le \sup A \).
~~~

!id: KPNEsJfCVd
!front: Prove the monotonicity of suprema: if \( A \subseteq B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) and both suprema exist, then \( \sup A \le \sup B \).
!back:
Let:

- \( A \subseteq B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \);
- \( \sup A \) and \( \sup B \) both exist.

To prove: \( \sup A \le \sup B \).

- Since \( \sup B \) is an upper bound of \( B \), it is also an upper bound of \( A \).
- Since \( \sup A \) is the least upper bound of \( A \), it is at most every upper bound of \( A \).
- Therefore \( \sup A \le \sup B \).
~~~

!id: HMc15ntjkw
!front: Prove the monotonicity of infima: if \( A \subseteq B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) and both infima exist, then \( \inf B \le \inf A \).
!back:
Let:

- \( A \subseteq B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \);
- \( \inf A \) and \( \inf B \) both exist.

To prove: \( \inf B \le \inf A \).

- Since \( \inf B \) is a lower bound of \( B \), it is also a lower bound of \( A \).
- Since \( \inf A \) is the greatest lower bound of \( A \), it is at least every lower bound of \( A \).
- Therefore \( \inf B \le \inf A \).
~~~

!id: ADZbAAAJFz
!front: How are \( \inf A \) and \( \sup(-A) \) related for a nonempty set \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) bounded below?
!back:
Let
\[ -A=\{-a:a\in A\}. \]

If \( A \) is nonempty and bounded below, then \( -A \) is nonempty and bounded above, and
\[ \inf A = -\sup(-A). \]

Lower bounds of \( A \) turn into upper bounds of \( -A \) by multiplying inequalities by \( -1 \).
~~~

!id: kReGA8We2L
!front: Derive \( \sup(A+B)=\sup A+\sup B \) for nonempty bounded-above subsets \( A,B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
Let:

- \( A,B \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) be nonempty and bounded above;
- \( A+B=\{a+b:a\in A,\ b\in B\} \);
- \( \alpha=\sup A \) and \( \beta=\sup B \).

To prove: \( \sup(A+B)=\alpha+\beta \).

- For every \( a\in A \) and \( b\in B \), \( a\le\alpha \) and \( b\le\beta \), so \( a+b\le\alpha+\beta \). Thus \( \alpha+\beta \) is an upper bound of \( A+B \).
- Given \( \varepsilon>0 \), choose \( a\in A \) with \( \alpha-\varepsilon/2<a \) and \( b\in B \) with \( \beta-\varepsilon/2<b \).
- Then \( a+b\in A+B \) and \( \alpha+\beta-\varepsilon<a+b \).
- Therefore elements of \( A+B \) lie arbitrarily close below \( \alpha+\beta \), so \( \sup(A+B)=\alpha+\beta \).
~~~
